            "text": "{\"study_topic_id\":\"study-51ef677b7c258bf64ba3\",\"overview\":{\"text\":\"Auguste Rodin (1840–1917) was a French sculptor whose expressive, emotionally charged work made him a major figure in modern art. He used bodies and gestures to convey thought, strain, and individual character, and challenged familiar conventions for public monuments. Among his best-known works are The Thinker and The Burghers of Calais. The Thinker began as The Poet, a figure of Dante intended for The Gates of Hell; Rodin later showed it independently and enlarged it. In The Burghers of Calais, six men face a shared fate as a group, while their distinct poses convey different emotions. Rodin also reworked figures from larger compositions into independent works.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.metmuseum.org/fr/essays/auguste-rodin-1840-1917\",\"https://www.musee-rodin.fr/en/musee/collections/oeuvres/penseur-meudon\",\"https://www.musee-rodin.fr/en/musee/collections/oeuvres/monument-burghers-calais\"]},\"key_facts\":[{\"text\":\"Rodin was born in Paris in 1840 and died in 1917.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.metmuseum.org/fr/essays/auguste-rodin-1840-1917\"]},{\"text\":\"The Thinker was first conceived in 1880 as The Poet, representing Dante above The Gates of Hell; Rodin later developed it into an independent work.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.musee-rodin.fr/en/musee/collections/oeuvres/penseur-meudon\"]},{\"text\":\"Rodin enlarged The Thinker in 1904; its powerful body contrasts with its pensive pose.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.musee-rodin.fr/en/musee/collections/oeuvres/penseur-meudon\"]},{\"text\":\"In The Burghers of Calais, six figures form a group, but each has an individual character and emotional expression.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.musee-rodin.fr/en/musee/collections/oeuvres/monument-burghers-calais\"]},{\"text\":\"Rodin isolated, modified, and recombined figures developed for The Gates of Hell, turning some into separate sculptures.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.metmuseum.org/fr/essays/auguste-rodin-1840-1917\"]}],\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"The Thinker\",\"url\":\"https://www.musee-rodin.fr/en/musee/collections/oeuvres/penseur-meudon\",\"publisher\":\"Musée Rodin\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:32:49.929218+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Rodin's dates; The Thinker's original title, connection to Dante and The Gates of Hell, later independent display and enlargement, and the contrast between its pensive pose and powerful body.\"},{\"title\":\"Monument to the Burghers of Calais\",\"url\":\"https://www.musee-rodin.fr/en/musee/collections/oeuvres/monument-burghers-calais\",\"publisher\":\"Musée Rodin\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:32:49.929218+00:00\",\"supports\":\"The six figures' shared composition and Rodin's attention to each individual's character and emotions.\"},{\"title\":\"Auguste Rodin (1840–1917)\",\"url\":\"https://www.metmuseum.org/fr/essays/auguste-rodin-1840-1917\",\"publisher\":\"The Metropolitan Museum of Art\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:32:49.929218+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Rodin's career and significance, innovations in public monuments, and his practice of isolating, modifying, and recombining figures from The Gates of Hell.\"}],\"research_queries\":[\"site:musee-rodin.fr/en Auguste Rodin biography The Thinker Gates of Hell\",\"site:metmuseum.org Auguste Rodin sculptor French 1840 1917 Burghers of Calais\",\"site:musee-rodin.fr/en \\\"Burghers of Calais\\\" Rodin monument\"],\"notes\":\"Overview is 101 words. Claims are linked to pages opened and read in this run; each key fact has supporting source URLs. The Burghers' historic episode is described only as a shared fate, without presenting details of the traditional account as independently verified history.\",\"unresolved_concerns\":[],\"self_check_complete\":true}"
